WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.681 --> 00:00:02.752
Hello!

00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:06.000
I'm Artem Vorozhtsov and this is the channel "Mathematical Experiments."

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.368
Last time, I started talking about the Legendre symbol but didn't fully explain the topic...

00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:15.716
To be honest, it's practically impossible to fully explain this topic. It's as inexhaustible as an electron.

00:00:15.716 --> 00:00:19.498
It has a vast continuation. There are various reciprocity laws,

00:00:19.498 --> 00:00:20.805
the quadratic reciprocity law,

00:00:20.805 --> 00:00:23.113
and the reciprocity law of Artin.

00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:28.377
And it extends all the way to modular forms, which is modern mathematics.

00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:31.471
Let's take it step by step. Gradually.

00:00:31.471 --> 00:00:35.318
So, last time, we defined three functions:

00:00:35.318 --> 00:00:39.712
PowP, SignP and LegendreSymbol.

00:00:39.712 --> 00:00:42.000
What were their definitions?

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.377
This function is simply the power of the number "m."

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:49.297
The power of (EulerPhi[p])/2. Euler's function divided by two.

00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:52.903
For prime numbers, Euler's function is equal to p - 1.

00:00:52.903 --> 00:00:58.636
So, for prime numbers, it's simply m^((p-1)/2).

00:00:58.636 --> 00:01:09.296
SignP (from the word "SignatureP") is the parity of the permutation generated by multiplying by the number "m" in the multiplication table modulo "p."

00:01:09.296 --> 00:01:16.431
And, naturally, we take the multiplication table modulo "p" only for numbers that are coprime with "p."

00:01:16.431 --> 00:01:21.918
And the Legendre symbol, which is equal to 1 or -1 depending on

00:01:21.918 --> 00:01:25.491
whether we can extract a square root of the number "m" modulo "p" or not.

00:01:25.491 --> 00:01:29.782
In other words, whether there is a number "x" whose square is equal to "m" or not.

00:01:29.782 --> 00:01:38.207
And we have seen that these three functions are equal if the number "p" is prime.

00:01:38.207 --> 00:01:46.163
I used the JacobiSymbol function, which is built into Wolfram Mathematica.

00:01:46.163 --> 00:01:54.000
This function is an extension of the Legendre symbol for non-prime cases of "p."

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.493
Wikipedia says that this extension is only for odd "p",

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:03.405
but in Wolfram Mathematica, this function is defined for even "p" as well.

00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:06.000
Here's how the definition works.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.179
JacobiSymbol is equal to LegendreSymbol for prime "p".

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:19.502
Additionally, for composite values of the second argument, we simply factorize it as a product of two Jacobi symbols.

00:02:19.502 --> 00:02:22.758
And so on. Recursively. Down to prime factors.

00:02:22.758 --> 00:02:29.430
In other words, JacobiSymbol is a multiplicative function with respect to the second argument.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:39.916
Also, for the case of odd "m" and "n", there is an extension of the Jacobi symbol for even second arguments.

00:02:39.916 --> 00:02:45.922
And of course, JacobiSymbol is also multiplicative with respect to the first argument.

00:02:45.922 --> 00:02:50.083
We can define JacobiSymbol in this way:

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:55.364
factorize the number "n"

00:02:55.364 --> 00:03:00.752
and for each factor, call the LegendreSymbol function.

00:03:00.752 --> 00:03:02.473
Let's do that.

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:07.940
So... To explain what's happening here, let's see the result of the FactorInteger function.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:13.564
It simply outputs a list of (prime number, power) pairs.

00:03:13.564 --> 00:03:19.892
If we apply "@@@" and the function to this result...

00:03:19.892 --> 00:03:24.665
Let me write some non-existent function here.

00:03:24.665 --> 00:03:26.263
Here's what we get.

00:03:26.263 --> 00:03:27.824
Once again...

00:03:27.824 --> 00:03:34.680
Without this addition, we get a list of prime factors with their powers,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:43.056
but with this addition, the prime number becomes the second argument, and the exponent becomes the exponent.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:53.007
We also need to replace the comma with a multiplication sign, and we do that with "Times @@".

00:03:53.007 --> 00:03:58.137
Well, that's the definition of this MyJacobiSymbol function.

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:01.373
Let's check if this definition works.

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:04.862
So I'll choose the number "m". Let's say 15.

00:04:04.862 --> 00:04:12.614
And I'll compare it for all odd values of the second argument.

00:04:12.614 --> 00:04:15.914
Let's try 3: True.

00:04:15.914 --> 00:04:18.774
Let's try 5: True. All good.

00:04:18.774 --> 00:04:23.070
In general... trust me.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:23.707
So...

00:04:23.707 --> 00:04:30.968
Next, I wanted to define another version of the PowP function, but with an added letter "n",

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:36.559
which equals zero if "m" and "p" are not coprime.

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:38.384
I already forgot why I did this...

00:04:38.384 --> 00:04:40.384
I wrote this file here.

00:04:40.384 --> 00:04:45.535
Let's set the Listable attribute for all these four functions like this,

00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:48.190
so that we can substitute a list as an argument.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:49.593
Like I'm doing here.

00:04:49.593 --> 00:04:53.679
I substituted a list as the first argument

00:04:53.679 --> 00:05:00.535
and then this whole expression becomes a list of values for the PowPn functions,

00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:05.647
where each element from the list is taken as the first argument.

00:05:05.647 --> 00:05:14.932
Here I'm comparing: is it true that the PowPn function matches the SignP function.

00:05:14.932 --> 00:05:20.152
Is it true that the SignP function matches the LegendreSymbol function.

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.733
And so on... you see, right?

00:05:22.733 --> 00:05:26.479
Here we get True, False, False... but let's start with something simple.

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:28.171
For example, the number 3.

00:05:28.171 --> 00:05:34.446
Let's make sure everything is fine. For the prime number 3, all four functions

00:05:34.446 --> 00:05:41.563
(PowPn, SignP, LegendreSymbol, and JacobiSymbol) give the same result.

00:05:41.563 --> 00:05:46.040
Let's try 37...

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.205
137

00:05:48.205 --> 00:05:49.658
127

00:05:49.658 --> 00:05:55.894
When you substitute a prime number, all four definitions give the same answer.

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:59.710
Now let's try 3 times 5.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.091
That's it. The situation is worse now.

00:06:02.091 --> 00:06:10.049
Only PowPn matches SignP. And all the others don't match each other.

00:06:10.049 --> 00:06:12.521
3 squared...

00:06:12.521 --> 00:06:16.641
They match each other, but not JacobiSymbol.

00:06:16.641 --> 00:06:18.531
And raised to the power of three...

00:06:18.531 --> 00:06:20.429
Again, they all match.

00:06:20.429 --> 00:06:25.989
It seems that for even powers, there are discrepancies,

00:06:25.989 --> 00:06:29.665
but for odd powers, all 4 functions match completely.

00:06:29.665 --> 00:06:33.961
Let's take 7 instead of 3...

00:06:33.961 --> 00:06:36.645
Too large of a number...

00:06:36.645 --> 00:06:44.361
Well... it turns out that for 5 squared, we only have a match for the first 3 functions,

00:06:44.361 --> 00:06:52.426
But for 5 cubed, all 4 functions match.

00:06:52.426 --> 00:07:00.716
Well then... essentially, we have three functions: PowPn and SignP always match.

00:07:00.716 --> 00:07:03.054
So we can consider them as one function.

00:07:03.054 --> 00:07:09.741
And we have PowPn, LegendreSymbol, and JacobiSymbol.

00:07:09.741 --> 00:07:13.217
Three functions. And we can study all three of them.

00:07:13.217 --> 00:07:18.364
But before we move on to studying JacobiSymbol, I want to say the following:

00:07:18.364 --> 00:07:24.336
because we can take this number field modulo a prime number "p" and add the square root of minus one to it,

00:07:24.336 --> 00:07:28.798
just like what we did when inventing complex numbers.

00:07:28.798 --> 00:07:30.803
And, in principle, it's a simple thing - you take it and add,

00:07:30.803 --> 00:07:36.536
and then everything happens automatically without your active participation.

00:07:36.536 --> 00:07:42.698
For example, let's take (3 + 5i) times (2 - i), where i is the square root of minus one.

00:07:42.698 --> 00:07:45.074
Yes, that's what we did...

00:07:45.074 --> 00:07:46.579
And what do we get...

00:07:46.579 --> 00:07:48.355
We just expand the brackets.

00:07:48.355 --> 00:07:50.629
i squared becomes -1.

00:07:50.629 --> 00:07:55.106
And where "i" remains as "i," we leave it as "i."

00:07:55.106 --> 00:08:01.591
Here we have 7i, because it's 5i times 2 minus 3i.

00:08:01.591 --> 00:08:04.892
And it becomes 10i minus 3i equals 7i.

00:08:04.892 --> 00:08:09.017
Try expanding these brackets yourself and recall at some point that "i^2" is equal to -1.

00:08:09.017 --> 00:08:12.322
Well, in addition, you can add the modulus to this.

00:08:12.322 --> 00:08:16.960
Take it modulo 7. And then it will simply be -3.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.393
Or take it modulo 5.

00:08:21.393 --> 00:08:22.953
You can also divide.

00:08:22.953 --> 00:08:24.958
Here, for example, the result of division.

00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:28.570
But we can take the result of division modulo 7.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:33.208
Why? Because 1/5 modulo 7 is actually 3.

00:08:33.208 --> 00:08:38.496
Indeed, 3 times 5 is 15, and 15 is exactly 1.

00:08:38.496 --> 00:08:44.103
So, 3 and 1/5 match in modular arithmetic modulo 7.

00:08:44.103 --> 00:08:50.047
Therefore, 13/5 also coincides with minus three, because 13 is equal to -1.

00:08:50.047 --> 00:08:53.176
In essence, we need to multiply -1 by 3.

00:08:53.176 --> 00:08:54.280
Here we go...

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:59.596
PolynomialMod is a function that can do everything that the Mod function can do and more.

00:08:59.596 --> 00:09:10.053
It can convert these proper fractions (m/n) into residues modulo p.

00:09:10.053 --> 00:09:11.095
Alright...

00:09:11.095 --> 00:09:13.669
Well, by the way, here's a remark.

00:09:13.669 --> 00:09:16.563
If I put 5 here, what will happen?

00:09:16.563 --> 00:09:19.526
Here's the answer: 1/5 plus 13i/5.

00:09:19.526 --> 00:09:23.252
But five is absent as a number in modular arithmetic modulo 5.

00:09:23.252 --> 00:09:26.722
Indeed.

00:09:26.722 --> 00:09:27.760
Well, what can we say here...

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.273
First of all, the number -1...

00:09:30.273 --> 00:09:34.667
From the number -1, we extract the square root in modular arithmetic modulo 5.

00:09:34.667 --> 00:09:35.504
It is 2.

00:09:35.504 --> 00:09:37.027
2^2 equals 4.

00:09:37.027 --> 00:09:38.788
And 4 is equal to -1.

00:09:38.788 --> 00:09:43.837
Because when we added √-1 in modular arithmetic modulo 5,

00:09:43.837 --> 00:09:45.942
we didn't actually add anything.

00:09:45.942 --> 00:09:52.222
In modular arithmetic modulo 5, this i (√-1) is simply 2.

00:09:52.222 --> 00:09:58.108
However, there is no number in modular arithmetic whose square equals three.

00:09:58.108 --> 00:09:59.800
You can check it yourself.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:09.500
Therefore, in modular arithmetic modulo 5, we can add i = √3.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:15.228
Let me define the symbol i in this way.

00:10:15.228 --> 00:10:18.498
Here...

00:10:18.498 --> 00:10:23.302
I will write PolynomialMod.

00:10:23.302 --> 00:10:30.261
Look, we know how to multiply numbers in modular arithmetic modulo 5,

00:10:30.261 --> 00:10:35.613
where we added √3, which is not present in the original number field.

00:10:35.613 --> 00:10:40.730
And now, let's learn division...

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.000
Here's what we get.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.595
He divided, but didn't finish it completely.

00:10:43.595 --> 00:10:47.829
Now we need to multiply it by 3 - √3.

00:10:47.829 --> 00:10:51.681
And multiply both the numerator and the denominator by this number and complete the calculations.

00:10:51.681 --> 00:10:55.364
I just made a note that

00:10:55.364 --> 00:11:02.973
the number field modulo "p" can be easily extended to a field with not "p" elements, but p^2.

00:11:02.973 --> 00:11:09.478
You just need to add √m, where m is some quadratic non-residue.

00:11:09.478 --> 00:11:16.887
That is, a residue for which there is no number x whose square is equal to it.

00:11:16.887 --> 00:11:20.984
Well, by the way, here we can make such an analogy:

00:11:20.984 --> 00:11:24.000
the Legendre symbol is either 1 or -1.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.747
By the way, it can be defined as 0 at the point 0.

00:11:27.747 --> 00:11:30.332
And then it can be perceived as the sign of a number.

00:11:30.332 --> 00:11:34.350
Indeed, it equals 1 for numbers from which the square root can be extracted.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:37.547
Just like in the case of real numbers.

00:11:37.547 --> 00:11:39.479
We can extract the square root of positive numbers.

00:11:39.479 --> 00:11:44.967
Therefore, those numbers for which the Legendre symbol equals 1,

00:11:44.967 --> 00:11:48.000
we can call them positive,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.040
and the other half of non-zero numbers we can call negative.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:55.912
Essentially, to extend the number field modulo "p",

00:11:55.912 --> 00:12:01.332
we need to add the square root of some negative number.

00:12:01.332 --> 00:12:03.954
Let's move on.

00:12:03.954 --> 00:12:09.696
We need to understand what the Legendre symbol equals for prime numbers.

00:12:09.696 --> 00:12:14.252
In the case when the number "m" (the first argument) equals -1.

00:12:14.252 --> 00:12:17.283
Here's what we get.

00:12:17.283 --> 00:12:21.027
These are prime numbers. This is the first element in the pairs.

00:12:21.027 --> 00:12:24.000
And the second number is precisely the value of the Legendre symbol,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:31.222
which coincides with the Jacobi symbol for the case of prime second arguments.

00:12:31.222 --> 00:12:42.323
Let's see for which prime numbers the Legendre symbol equals -1 and for which it equals 1.

00:12:42.323 --> 00:12:43.274
Roughly evenly.

00:12:43.274 --> 00:12:45.074
We can even specify.

00:12:45.074 --> 00:12:46.497
Here, there are 48 numbers.

00:12:46.497 --> 00:12:52.669
So there are 52 numbers here, because I initially took just 100 numbers.

00:12:52.669 --> 00:12:54.263
Good!

00:12:54.263 --> 00:13:00.000
In general, there is a sense of balance.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.000
As if half of the numbers.

00:13:05.215 --> 00:13:12.414
For half of the prime numbers, -1 is positive, and for the other half, it's negative.

00:13:12.414 --> 00:13:15.513
See? 504 is close to half.

00:13:15.513 --> 00:13:18.000
By the way, it's interesting to study this balance. How it changes.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.417
So we will increase the number "m". Look at all prime numbers less than "m" and see,

00:13:23.417 --> 00:13:30.000
how many prime numbers we get there, for which -1 is negative.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.625
And how many for which -1 is positive.

00:13:35.625 --> 00:13:38.378
Here is the answer to the question.

00:13:38.378 --> 00:13:42.841
It turns out we need to look at the remainder modulo 4.

00:13:42.841 --> 00:13:46.774
I would like in my videos for the thought to go by some natural path

00:13:46.774 --> 00:13:49.903
and not have such sudden illuminations.

00:13:49.903 --> 00:13:53.818
Not illuminations, more precisely, but ready answers.

00:13:53.818 --> 00:13:56.267
Well, you see...

00:13:56.267 --> 00:14:04.672
I think that, in any case, if you honestly looked at these numbers, you would guess.

00:14:04.672 --> 00:14:06.784
You would keep looking.

00:14:06.784 --> 00:14:10.596
And at some point, you would see this regularity.

00:14:10.596 --> 00:14:15.371
And, by the way, this theorem is easy to prove.

00:14:15.371 --> 00:14:16.597
It is proven as follows:

00:14:16.597 --> 00:14:24.787
if "p" (a prime number) has a remainder of 1 when divided by four, then (-1)^(p-1)/2 equals 1.

00:14:24.787 --> 00:14:28.433
Because (p-1)/2 is an even number.

00:14:28.433 --> 00:14:32.575
In general, in this case, everything turned out to be very simple.

00:14:32.575 --> 00:14:40.052
So, we have the assumption that the number of prime numbers with a remainder of 1 modulo 4

00:14:40.052 --> 00:14:48.479
is approximately the same as the number of prime numbers with a remainder of -1 modulo 4, no matter how large "n" we take,

00:14:48.479 --> 00:14:52.400
which limits our prime numbers.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:54.000
What does "approximately the same" mean?

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.445
We will discuss that today.

00:14:56.445 --> 00:15:03.887
What is the value of the Legendre symbol if we take 2 as the first argument?

00:15:03.887 --> 00:15:09.854
Let's see for which numbers the Legendre symbol for 2 equals -1.

00:15:09.854 --> 00:15:13.632
Here, the numbers themselves are not present, only Mod is taken.

00:15:13.632 --> 00:15:16.527
Let me temporarily remove Mod.

00:15:16.527 --> 00:15:20.203
These prime numbers for which 2 is a negative number.

00:15:20.203 --> 00:15:27.231
Let's check if the answer lies again in the residue modulo 4.

00:15:27.231 --> 00:15:28.752
But no...

00:15:28.752 --> 00:15:32.188
Here, all reasonable residues are present.

00:15:32.188 --> 00:15:34.154
Both 1 and 3. There are only 2 of them.

00:15:34.154 --> 00:15:36.385
No luck!

00:15:38.061 --> 00:15:41.425
Alright... Let's try 8.

00:15:41.425 --> 00:15:47.529
And here we got a similar answer.

00:15:47.529 --> 00:15:48.655
Look...

00:15:48.655 --> 00:15:55.238
Residues for prime numbers for which 2 is negative are 3 and 5,

00:15:55.238 --> 00:16:03.570
and for those for which 2 is positive, in the sense of taking the square root, residues are 1 and 7.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:06.562
That's the hypothesis.

00:16:06.562 --> 00:16:09.798
Let's move on.

00:16:09.798 --> 00:16:11.822
So, here's our answer for 2.

00:16:11.822 --> 00:16:13.685
And for 3?

00:16:13.685 --> 00:16:14.898
Here's the answer for 3.

00:16:14.898 --> 00:16:18.564
I tried three first,

00:16:18.564 --> 00:16:20.847
but it didn't work...

00:16:20.847 --> 00:16:25.991
Both sets had both residues. 1 and 2.

00:16:25.991 --> 00:16:30.150
Then I tried 6.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.150
And it didn't work either.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:33.876
The same set of residues.

00:16:33.876 --> 00:16:40.373
That means it doesn't allow us to divide into two parts.

00:16:40.373 --> 00:16:46.203
But 12 worked.

00:16:46.203 --> 00:16:49.089
1, 11 and 5, 7

00:16:49.089 --> 00:16:50.268
Let's continue.

00:16:50.268 --> 00:16:54.978
For 5, it simply worked with 5.

00:16:54.978 --> 00:17:01.185
For 7, we had to multiply by 4 again.

00:17:01.185 --> 00:17:05.702
For 11, we also had to multiply by 4.

00:17:05.702 --> 00:17:07.879
Here's 13.

00:17:07.879 --> 00:17:09.161
No need to multiply by 4.

00:17:09.161 --> 00:17:10.995
17

00:17:10.995 --> 00:17:13.376
29

00:17:13.376 --> 00:17:19.737
But for 43, we had to multiply in order for these two sets of residues to be different, with no intersection.

00:17:19.737 --> 00:17:21.976
So...

00:17:21.976 --> 00:17:29.111
When we substitute "m" as the first argument (some prime number),

00:17:29.111 --> 00:17:38.942
the answer to the question of what the Legendre symbol equals depends on the remainder of the second argument divided

00:17:38.942 --> 00:17:40.105
by this number

00:17:40.105 --> 00:17:42.105
or multiplying this number by 4.

00:17:42.105 --> 00:17:44.452
And here's what we've got.

00:17:44.452 --> 00:17:49.120
The Jacobi symbol, when the first argument is fixed,

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:51.462
is a periodic function with respect to the second argument,

00:17:51.462 --> 00:17:58.148
where the period is either the value of the first argument or 4 multiplied by the value of the first argument.

00:17:58.148 --> 00:18:05.830
And if you look closely, you'll understand that it depends on

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:10.327
which number "p" is substituted as the first argument.

00:18:10.327 --> 00:18:12.865
A number that has a remainder of 1 modulo 4

00:18:12.865 --> 00:18:16.301
or a remainder of 3 modulo 4.

00:18:16.301 --> 00:18:18.921
Here, 43 has a remainder of 3 modulo 4.

00:18:18.921 --> 00:18:22.687
That's why we had to multiply by 4,

00:18:22.687 --> 00:18:28.796
to get two non-overlapping sets of residues as a result.

00:18:28.796 --> 00:18:30.767
Let's continue.

00:18:30.767 --> 00:18:41.695
Now, I promised to show you the balance we have. And now we're getting to that.

00:18:41.695 --> 00:18:47.115
Let's try putting -1 and see this simple thing,

00:18:47.115 --> 00:18:53.805
that the Jacobi symbol, as we gradually increase the second argument, equals either 1 or -1,

00:18:53.805 --> 00:19:00.729
depending on the straightforward remainder modulo 4.

00:19:00.729 --> 00:19:04.508
Here I'm only going through odd numbers.

00:19:04.508 --> 00:19:11.198
We can remove "Accumulate" for now. It's not needed here.

00:19:11.198 --> 00:19:16.105
And make sure that JacobiSymbol equals either 1 or -1.

00:19:16.105 --> 00:19:21.422
Alright. Let's bring back "Accumulate"...

00:19:21.422 --> 00:19:25.320
Let's see for the number 43.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:29.418
Here we can see precisely this periodicity in the Accumulate function.

00:19:29.418 --> 00:19:32.273
The period is equal to 4 multiplied by 43, presumably.

00:19:32.273 --> 00:19:34.220
We can verify that.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:39.982
What is the distance between this vertex and this one? We just need to study this moment.

00:19:39.982 --> 00:19:42.705
Alright... Good.

00:19:42.705 --> 00:19:45.765
Moving on.

00:19:45.765 --> 00:19:55.028
And if we take not the JacobiSymbol but our function PowP, what then?

00:19:55.028 --> 00:19:56.090
Hmm...

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.329
Here the situation is more complicated.

00:19:58.329 --> 00:20:02.244
I'll remove the division I did here...

00:20:02.244 --> 00:20:07.669
Here we get that the Accumulate of our function is this kind of function

00:20:07.669 --> 00:20:10.045
that constantly increases its amplitude.

00:20:10.045 --> 00:20:13.755
And to suppress this increase in amplitude,

00:20:13.755 --> 00:20:22.116
we need to divide by the number "n" that we took as the second argument.

00:20:22.116 --> 00:20:29.743
And then we get this function confined within some corridor.

00:20:29.743 --> 00:20:30.683
So...

00:20:30.683 --> 00:20:33.812
Something doesn't sit right with me. There are no GridLines here.

00:20:33.812 --> 00:20:36.907
We need to add them. SetOptions...

00:20:36.907 --> 00:20:47.354
for the ListPlot function, as well as for the Plot function, we need to set GridLines -&gt; Automatic.

00:20:47.354 --> 00:20:49.617
There we go...

00:20:49.617 --> 00:20:56.735
If we remove Accumulate...

00:20:56.735 --> 00:20:59.009
then we'll see this kind of unclear plot.

00:20:59.009 --> 00:21:05.127
That's why I use Accumulate everywhere, to make the plot clearer.

00:21:05.127 --> 00:21:13.052
It seems like Accumulate adds another operation and complicates your understanding of what you see.

00:21:13.052 --> 00:21:14.510
But no...

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:17.617
The function Accumulate calculates the balance of ones and minus ones.

00:21:17.617 --> 00:21:22.250
But, by the way, the function PowerP does not necessarily return only ones and minus ones.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:25.760
It is simply "m" raised to the power of half of the Euler function.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:34.516
We still need to see what it equals when we substitute some non-prime values for the second argument.

00:21:34.516 --> 00:21:37.521
And we will definitely take a look.

00:21:37.521 --> 00:21:46.092
But first, let's see how things are with the analogous function for the SignP function.

00:21:46.092 --> 00:21:50.594
Here, we get such a growing graph.

00:21:50.594 --> 00:21:54.205
Clearly, JacobiSymbol is the most regular thing

00:21:54.205 --> 00:21:58.938
and perhaps we should start studying it first.

00:21:58.938 --> 00:22:00.000
So...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.150
We have understood,

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:08.075
that the JacobiSymbol function is periodic both in the first and second arguments.

00:22:08.075 --> 00:22:13.940
And we have a hypothesis about the period in the second argument.

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:20.911
It equals 1 and -1 in the case when we take -1 as "m".

00:22:20.911 --> 00:22:25.095
When we take the Accumulate of it, nothing interesting happens.

00:22:25.095 --> 00:22:28.160
But let's take prime numbers.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:32.597
We will go not through odd numbers, but through prime numbers

00:22:32.597 --> 00:22:34.579
or their powers.

00:22:34.579 --> 00:22:38.973
And let's see what the balance of ones and minus ones will be.

00:22:38.973 --> 00:22:46.673
How this balance will change with the growth of a certain limit number N.

00:22:46.673 --> 00:22:47.647
So...

00:22:47.647 --> 00:22:50.759
Accumulate is growing.

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:55.324
It looks like the square root of x on average.

00:22:55.324 --> 00:22:56.059
Good.

00:22:56.059 --> 00:23:00.248
Let's divide our "y" by "√x".

00:23:00.248 --> 00:23:02.009
Here's how it's done.

00:23:02.009 --> 00:23:06.000
Yes, by the way, let's take the logarithm of "x".

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.274
That's done here.

00:23:08.274 --> 00:23:09.727
So...

00:23:11.163 --> 00:23:14.702
The x-axis is already the logarithm of the number "n".

00:23:14.702 --> 00:23:24.363
The value of "y" is the sum of JacobiSymbol values up to the number "x" (or rather the exponentiation of "x")

00:23:24.363 --> 00:23:28.728
over all powers of prime numbers that are less than "x".

00:23:28.728 --> 00:23:38.003
Not just over all powers of prime numbers, but over the powers of prime numbers that are not equal to two.

00:23:38.003 --> 00:23:41.022
Two is a special case, you remember, right?

00:23:41.022 --> 00:23:48.801
Let's take a million numbers and find among them those that are powers of odd prime numbers.

00:23:48.801 --> 00:23:52.682
And we really want to continue this graph.

00:23:52.682 --> 00:23:55.947
Extend it to the right.

00:23:55.947 --> 00:23:59.388
So... we've drawn it.

00:23:59.388 --> 00:24:02.085
There it is.

00:24:02.085 --> 00:24:04.363
Hypothesis:

00:24:04.363 --> 00:24:10.398
This function is within a corridor that it doesn't leave.

00:24:10.398 --> 00:24:17.054
From 0 to 0.2, starting from some large value of "x".

00:24:17.054 --> 00:24:22.709
Let's see what values occur on the y-axis in this graph.

00:24:22.709 --> 00:24:26.150
To do that, let's plot a histogram of the "y" values.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:28.009
Here it is.

00:24:28.009 --> 00:24:36.171
It would be nice to extend it further and see what this distribution tends to.

00:24:36.171 --> 00:24:37.520
Moving on.

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:45.339
Here it calculated all the powers of odd prime numbers less than 3 million in 30 seconds.

00:24:45.339 --> 00:24:47.795
Then I wrote the ExpSlice function,

00:24:47.795 --> 00:24:54.412
which allows me to take only a subset of "x, y" pairs when I plot graphs.

00:24:54.412 --> 00:24:56.191
So... Here I...

00:24:56.191 --> 00:24:56.960
I wrote a long time ago.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:03.102
Here, I tried to write an optimized version of the function that calculates all the powers of odd prime numbers.

00:25:03.937 --> 00:25:05.829
Well, alright...

00:25:05.829 --> 00:25:07.932
Let's not use it.

00:25:07.932 --> 00:25:14.994
Let's calculate the Accumulate of JacobiSymbol and powers of prime numbers.

00:25:14.994 --> 00:25:22.744
And let's plot the graph, taking only 50 thousand points from it,

00:25:22.744 --> 00:25:26.007
which are evenly distributed on this logarithmic axis.

00:25:26.007 --> 00:25:33.405
ExpSlice is precisely about taking 50 thousand points evenly distributed on the logarithmic axis.

00:25:33.405 --> 00:25:38.899
Now let's figure out the PowP function.

00:25:38.899 --> 00:25:45.042
Here are the values it takes for the first 500 odd numbers.

00:25:45.042 --> 00:25:50.296
You can see that 5, 15, 10 occur here.

00:25:50.296 --> 00:25:53.048
Well, alright...

00:25:53.048 --> 00:25:56.191
Let's plot...

00:25:56.191 --> 00:26:01.308
So... let's first look at a large number of numbers in the figure.

00:26:01.308 --> 00:26:03.997
You can see that there are two harmonics here.

00:26:03.997 --> 00:26:05.284
One is fast,

00:26:05.284 --> 00:26:09.986
and the other is slow, constantly increasing in amplitude.

00:26:09.986 --> 00:26:13.662
Well, and the amplitude of the first harmonic also increases.

00:26:13.662 --> 00:26:15.773
Alright.

00:26:15.773 --> 00:26:20.270
Let's calculate all this further.

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:22.925
10 thousand

00:26:22.925 --> 00:26:24.348
So...

00:26:24.348 --> 00:26:28.371
Let's divide y by x.

00:26:28.371 --> 00:26:29.487
So...

00:26:29.487 --> 00:26:32.485
Here's another hypothesis for you.

00:26:32.485 --> 00:26:35.798
This function doesn't leave a certain corridor.

00:26:35.798 --> 00:26:37.050
So...

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:40.542
But this is our function for PowP.

00:26:40.542 --> 00:26:42.884
And if we write "n" here,

00:26:42.884 --> 00:26:48.821
then in the places where these numbers were greater than one in absolute value, we simply get zero.

00:26:48.821 --> 00:26:55.829
These large jumps on this function are the cases when there were large numbers here.

00:26:55.829 --> 00:27:01.851
Now we've basically zeroed out all these large jumps and want to plot what we get.

00:27:01.851 --> 00:27:03.343
What do we see here?

00:27:03.343 --> 00:27:07.991
A growing function. Approximately linear.

00:27:07.991 --> 00:27:11.454
For 250,000, the answer is 80,000.

00:27:11.454 --> 00:27:14.724
I've prepared some options here.

00:27:14.724 --> 00:27:20.657
Let's subtract approximately 0.33 from "x".

00:27:20.657 --> 00:27:25.056
We get a negative function, indicating a crossover.

00:27:25.056 --> 00:27:29.382
We subtracted too much, but no worries.

00:27:29.382 --> 00:27:34.290
Let's add 10√x to it.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:36.000
Here's what we get.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:42.000
And from there, you can try adding some higher powers of "x",

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:45.710
but it's a thankless task.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:58.796
Let's ask Mathematica to fit the value of "y" as a sum of some components.

00:27:58.796 --> 00:28:00.522
Fit it like this, for example:

00:28:00.522 --> 00:28:09.041
I divided "y" by √x and started fitting this function.

00:28:09.041 --> 00:28:13.953
Let's see what we get.

00:28:13.953 --> 00:28:16.588
Well, not bad!

00:28:16.588 --> 00:28:18.000
This is the residual.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.762
The difference between "y" and our approximation of "y" using

00:28:23.762 --> 00:28:28.156
a linear combination of these power functions of "x".

00:28:28.156 --> 00:28:31.592
So... Is it important to have x^(1/4) here?

00:28:31.592 --> 00:28:35.028
Let's check.

00:28:35.028 --> 00:28:37.306
Yes. The residual deteriorates significantly.

00:28:37.306 --> 00:28:40.327
Alright. So it's needed here.

00:28:40.327 --> 00:28:41.575
Okay.

00:28:41.575 --> 00:28:45.413
Let's look at what it has fitted.

00:28:45.413 --> 00:28:49.191
Take a look at my reasoning on this topic.

00:28:49.191 --> 00:28:53.282
When I see a small coefficient, that's good.

00:28:53.282 --> 00:28:57.150
These numbers, which are less modulo than 10, are good numbers.

00:28:57.150 --> 00:29:02.130
They indicate that we have chosen good first three components.

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:07.208
And when the coefficients which are got on the regression line are large,

00:29:07.208 --> 00:29:11.602
there is a suspicion that the wrong basis of functions has been chosen.

00:29:11.602 --> 00:29:14.594
Well, most likely this basis needs to be changed.

00:29:14.594 --> 00:29:17.235
Okay. So we got the higher powers right,

00:29:17.235 --> 00:29:19.460
but we didn't quite get what's here.

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:27.431
Perhaps we need to choose a different basis with logarithms or something else.

00:29:27.431 --> 00:29:31.651
Well, be that as it may, here we need to solve two tasks.

00:29:31.651 --> 00:29:33.206
Come up with this basis,

00:29:33.206 --> 00:29:35.795
and secondly, formulate a theorem

00:29:35.795 --> 00:29:45.627
that after subtracting this approximation, the given function does not leave a certain corridor from -0.2 to 0.2.

00:29:45.627 --> 00:29:48.000
Okay.

00:29:56.208 --> 00:30:00.601
For m=43, we also get a good approximation, but the coefficients are different.

00:30:00.962 --> 00:30:03.859
Here, it seems, the same thing was done. Not interesting.

00:30:06.732 --> 00:30:12.761
So... What about the Signature?

00:30:12.761 --> 00:30:16.206
The same thing can be done for the Signature function.

00:30:16.206 --> 00:30:18.211
Here's what we get.

00:30:18.211 --> 00:30:24.000
Here, too, a certain ready-made answer is already drawn.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.163
A similar investigation can be conducted here.

00:30:27.163 --> 00:30:30.000
Okay. Let's consider another example.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:37.476
We have already plotted this Accumulate function of JacobiSymbol for the case when m = -1,

00:30:37.476 --> 00:30:42.559
and the second argument ranges over the powers of odd prime numbers.

00:30:42.559 --> 00:30:45.619
Such a growing function.

00:30:45.619 --> 00:31:04.296
And it turns out that we need to subtract approximately 0.09√x from it

00:31:04.296 --> 00:31:08.314
to dampen this growing square root.

00:31:08.314 --> 00:31:22.660
But if we write EulerPhi[#] here,

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:27.978
we will discover not a growing function, but a decreasing one.

00:31:27.978 --> 00:31:31.036
And also approximately according to the quadratic law.

00:31:31.036 --> 00:31:39.617
So we will need to add 0.09√x here.

00:31:39.617 --> 00:31:41.160
Here we go...

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:45.520
Food for thought, so to speak.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:47.075
And that's all for today!

00:31:47.075 --> 00:31:49.741
Good luck, everyone! Bye!

